course: dictionary
lesson: 6025
title: Meerkat
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A meerkat is a noun, a word for a thing. Here the thing is an animal. The dictionary gives you the meaning, but the sentences around the word add clues. «Meerkats stand up tall on their back legs as lookouts for hawks and jackals» tells you they watch for danger.
explain: Look for clue words like «guard», «lookouts» and «alarm». They show that the meerkat is not just standing; it is keeping the group safe. Ask yourself what would fit in the sentence: a watcher, a guard, a lookout.
explain: Plurals are clues too. «One meerkat stands guard» talks about one animal, while «meerkats dig all day» talks about many. So a meerkat becomes meerkats when there is more than one.
fact: A meerkat is a small, furry animal of the mongoose family.
fact: Meerkats live in groups in the deserts and dry grasslands of southern Africa.
fact: Meerkats are famous for standing up on their back legs to watch for danger.
fact: While one meerkat stands guard as a sentry, the others dig for insects, scorpions and lizards.
fact: In «mobs of up to about 50», the word mobs means groups of meerkats.
exercise: «Meerkats stand up tall on their back legs as lookouts for hawks and jackals.» What are meerkats doing when they stand up? | Swimming in a river | Sleeping in a burrow | Digging for beetles | *Watching for danger
exercise: In «one meerkat stands guard on a high spot», find and copy the word that tells you the job of the meerkat. | one | high | *guard | spot
exercise: Where do meerkats live? | *In deserts and dry grasslands of southern Africa | In the sea | In tall city flats | In the Arctic snow
test: What family do meerkats belong to? | Bear family | Cat family | Dog family | *Mongoose family
test: «Its alarm calls tell the others whether danger comes from the sky.» What do the alarm calls tell the others? | *Whether danger comes from the sky or the ground | That it is time to sleep | How to dig a burrow | Where the dinner is
exam: Which word is the plural of meerkat? | meerkatts | *meerkats | meerkates | meerkat
